WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:40.242 --> 00:00:45.520
[Narrator] Forensic science is all&nbsp;
around us. We see it in the news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:52.480
on our favorite television shows and read about it&nbsp;
in popular novels. It is part of our daily lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.560
When a life is unexpectedly extinguished,&nbsp;
we need answers and seek the cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.240
Today, this need is addressed in police&nbsp;
investigations, laboratories courtrooms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:15.200
all of the venues in which scientific medicine&nbsp;
interacts with the law, the field of forensics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.720
The National Library of Medicine presents&nbsp;
Visible Proofs: Forensic Views of the Body.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.000
An exhibition about the&nbsp;
history of forensic medicine.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.320
Over the centuries, physicians, surgeons and other&nbsp;
professionals have struggled to develop scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.200
methods that translate views of bodies and body&nbsp;
parts into visible proofs that can persuade judges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:50.240
juries and the public. [Fowler] Almost everything&nbsp;
is derived originally from pathology which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.360
if you look at the original word pathology, the&nbsp;
greek word, it's study of disease. [Fierro] All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.560
of medicine deals with the little mystery of&nbsp;
what happens to be wrong with the patient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.160
Now we come into the picture after this event&nbsp;
has already occurred the individual is dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.160
on the other hand the information that&nbsp;
we we gather from these dead people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.360
it has to, is our mission in&nbsp;
medicine, be used for prevention.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:22.080
[Narrator] Forensic Medicine also called&nbsp;
Medical Jurisprudence or Legal Medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.520
emerged in the 1600s, a time when physicians&nbsp;
and surgeons were becoming increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:34.960
involved in legal proceedings. In the late 1700s,&nbsp;
Medical Jurisprudence became part of the formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:41.680
medical curriculum. To win acceptance, aspiring&nbsp;
medical experts had to make their procedures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.080
accomplishments and themselves visible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.440
Today, forensic medicine increasingly relies&nbsp;
on DNA analysis, digital imaging and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.320
sophisticated technologies to identify victims,&nbsp;
convict the guilty and exonerate the innocent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.000
in criminal civil and governmental proceedings.&nbsp;
[Scheck] The great thing about forensic science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.760
in the criminal justice system is that we've&nbsp;
been able, certainly with DNA testing, not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.960
to protect the innocent but to do a better job of&nbsp;
going out there and apprehending those who really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.880
committed the crime. [Narrator] DNA analysis has&nbsp;
emerged as an indispensable method of identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.640
suspects and victims of crimes. [Fowler]&nbsp;
Everywhere we go we potentially leave a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:34.160
amount of DNA behind which can then be picked&nbsp;
up and examined in a laboratory and the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:42.400
nature of that DNA can then be compared to a&nbsp;
sample of DNA from a person who is a suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:48.640
at a scene. [Sherry] The role of DNA is a&nbsp;
fascinating impartial role because it speaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.360
with an impartiality, a fact it can exonerate the&nbsp;
innocent and it can implicate the truly guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.040
not per se in a value-laden way but just as a&nbsp;
statement of the material fact that this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.360
was at some place in time at which&nbsp;
point they left a trace of their DNA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:13.200
What we're discovering in the recent advances in&nbsp;
molecular biology and genetics is that we have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.440
unprecedented opportunity to identify people with&nbsp;
a precision to affiliate them correctly with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.800
family and and that's a new type of technology&nbsp;
and so it's raising expectations of closure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.560
it is providing a meaning and a value to&nbsp;
science in terms of how it can deliver an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.440
impact on the lives of everyone even&nbsp;
if they don't participate in research

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:42.800
[Narrator] Since the 17th century, narratives&nbsp;
have mixed violence and murder police and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:49.920
scientific investigation and courtroom drama and&nbsp;
have attracted a mass audience but the intensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.080
of public interest in forensics is now vastly&nbsp;
greater due to its prominence in the mass media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.280
as portrayed in the news and crime dramas.&nbsp;
[Sheck] Well, the American public knows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.640
most about forensic science from watching high&nbsp;
profile trials on television and these days from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:15.520
watching CSI and other you know police procedurals&nbsp;
and i have to tell you that forensic science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:25.920
is not CSI. It's not that new technologies don't&nbsp;
exist, they do, it's just that they don't exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.560
and they're not implemented in the ways that we&nbsp;
see on television. [Sherry] As fast as modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:36.240
sciences and the technology it's virtually&nbsp;
impossible to get the right answer with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:42.800
confidence the show purports in 60 minutes. So I&nbsp;
think exhibits like this are pulling together the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.000
whole growth in the uh the maturation of the field&nbsp;
where the science is taking us in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:57.120
CSI certainly you know holds up the excitement of&nbsp;
of a potential quick answer. We're not quite there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:03.120
yet. [Sheck] Well it's great to have an exhibition&nbsp;
that truly demonstrates to the public the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.840
science can change the way we do business in the&nbsp;
criminal justice system because it's interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:18.080
how it evolves. You have something like DNA&nbsp;
technology and it's a great advance but one of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:26.000
primary contributions is it shows the inadequacy&nbsp;
of some past technology. [Narrator] Visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.640
Proofs: Forensic Views of the Body, explores the&nbsp;
human endeavors in developing scientific methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:39.680
that translate views of bodies and body parts into&nbsp;
visible proofs. It tells stories of the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:46.360
sciences and technologies that make visible&nbsp;
the cause and manner behind a death. [Fierro]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:54.160
The patient is telling you what happened to him.&nbsp;
He's telling you a story. He is asking you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.880
figure out what happened to him and that you do&nbsp;
you have great respect for your patient whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:15.840
he's dead or whether he's alive and seek to&nbsp;
answer those questions that need to be answered.

